<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ §01 THE PROBLEM -->
<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Problem</div>
<h2>SFT's Hidden Gradient Distortion</h2>
<p>Fine-tuning on expert demonstrations seems simple: minimize cross-entropy on the correct tokens. But lurking inside this routine is a distortion that punishes exactly the wrong thing.</p>
<p>Consider training on a math proof that contains the token <strong>"hypotenuse"</strong>. If your model is uncertain about it — say, p = 0.08 — the cross-entropy loss's gradient pushes that token with a multiplier of 1/0.08 = <strong>12.5×</strong>. A rare symbol like "∑" at p = 0.02 gets pushed 50×. Meanwhile, common tokens like "the" at p = 0.92 barely move.</p>
<p>The result: training is dominated by rare, low-probability tokens. The model memorizes them at the expense of learning the reasoning pattern behind them. SFT memorizes; RL generalizes. The question is: can we fix SFT itself?</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">gradient weight vs. token probability — SFT vs. DFT</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click a token to highlight</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="weight-canvas" style="width:100%;height:240px;display:block"></canvas>
    <div class="token-cards" id="token-cards" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <div class="token-card card-safe" data-p="0.92" data-token="&quot;the&quot;">
        <div class="tc-token">"the"</div>
        <div class="tc-prob">p = 0.92</div>
        <div class="tc-weight-row">
          <div><span class="tc-sft">1.09×</span><span class="tc-label">SFT</span></div>
          <div><span class="tc-dft">1.00×</span><span class="tc-label">DFT</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="token-card card-warn" data-p="0.35" data-token="&quot;because&quot;">
        <div class="tc-token">"because"</div>
        <div class="tc-prob">p = 0.35</div>
        <div class="tc-weight-row">
          <div><span class="tc-sft">2.86×</span><span class="tc-label">SFT</span></div>
          <div><span class="tc-dft">1.00×</span><span class="tc-label">DFT</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="token-card card-danger" data-p="0.08" data-token="&quot;hypotenuse&quot;">
        <div class="tc-token">"hypotenuse"</div>
        <div class="tc-prob">p = 0.08</div>
        <div class="tc-weight-row">
          <div><span class="tc-sft">12.5×</span><span class="tc-label">SFT</span></div>
          <div><span class="tc-dft">1.00×</span><span class="tc-label">DFT</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="token-card card-critical" data-p="0.02" data-token="&quot;∑_&#123;k=1&#125;&quot;">
        <div class="tc-token">"∑_&#123;k=1&#125;"</div>
        <div class="tc-prob">p = 0.02</div>
        <div class="tc-weight-row">
          <div><span class="tc-sft">50×</span><span class="tc-label">SFT</span></div>
          <div><span class="tc-dft">1.00×</span><span class="tc-label">DFT</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The chart above shows the core problem in one glance. The red SFT curve (gradient weight = 1/p) explodes toward infinity for rare tokens, while DFT's green line stays flat at 1. Every token, regardless of how rare, contributes equally.</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ §02 THEORY -->
<section id="s-theory" data-code="theory">
<div class="section-no">02 — SFT as RL</div>
<h2>The Importance Sampling Proof</h2>
<p>The paper derives this analytically. The SFT gradient looks like an expectation over the training data, but with one algebraic rewrite it becomes an on-policy gradient — the same form as REINFORCE — with an implicit reward structure:</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">SFT gradient (standard form)</div>
  ∇<sub>θ</sub> L<sub>SFT</sub> = -𝔼<sub>(x,y*)</sub> [ ∇<sub>θ</sub> log π<sub>θ</sub>(y* | x) ]
</div>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">After importance sampling rewrite</div>
  = -𝔼<sub>x</sub> 𝔼<sub>y~π</sub> [ <span class="math-var">w(y|x)</span> · ∇<sub>θ</sub> log π<sub>θ</sub>(y|x) · <span class="math-var">r(x,y)</span> ]
  <br>
  where <span class="math-var">w(y|x) = 1 / π<sub>θ</sub>(y|x)</span>,  <span class="math-var">r(x,y) = 𝟙[y = y*]</span>
  <br>
  <span class="math-comment">← this is exactly the REINFORCE policy gradient with reward w·r</span>
</div>

<p>The reward the model is implicitly optimizing is <strong>1/π(y*|x)</strong> — inversely proportional to how confident the model already is. When the model is unsure (p is small), the reward explodes. When the model is confident (p is large), the reward is near 1. This creates two problems:</p>

<div class="step-list" id="theory-steps">
  <div class="step-box active" id="tstep-0">
    <div class="step-num">1</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Sparse reward signal</div>
      <div class="step-desc">The indicator r(x,y) = 𝟙[y = y*] is non-zero only for exact matches to the expert trajectory. There's no partial credit. The model gets one signal: "match this token perfectly."</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" id="tstep-1">
    <div class="step-num">2</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Explosive 1/p weighting</div>
      <div class="step-desc">The importance weight 1/π amplifies gradients for low-probability tokens. The rarer the token, the louder its gradient shouts over the rest of training. Optimization becomes unstable and biased toward memorizing rare patterns.</div>
      <div class="step-code">token "∑_&#123;k=1&#125;" at p=0.02 → gradient ×50 vs. "the" at p=0.92 → gradient ×1.09</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" id="tstep-2">
    <div class="step-num">3</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Why RL doesn't suffer this</div>
      <div class="step-desc">RL samples from the current policy, computes rewards, and applies gradients uniformly weighted by those rewards — no 1/p distortion. The reward is dense (applies to all sampled responses) and does not invert model confidence.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="step-nav">
  <button id="theory-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
  <button id="theory-next">Next →</button>
  <span class="step-prog" id="theory-prog">Step 1 of 3</span>
</div>

<p>This analysis isn't just theoretical framing — it directly points to the fix. The distortion comes from the 1/π weight. Cancel it.</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ §03 DFT -->
<section id="s-dft" data-code="dft">
<div class="section-no">03 — DFT: One Line</div>
<h2>Reward Rectification: Multiply by p</h2>
<p>The fix is elegant: multiply the loss by π<sub>θ</sub>(y*|x) at each token position, with a stop-gradient so the weight doesn't backpropagate through itself. This cancels the 1/p distortion, making the effective reward uniformly 1 for all tokens.</p>

<div class="loss-diff">
  <div class="loss-box sft">
    <div class="loss-box-head">Standard SFT</div>
    <div class="loss-box-body">
      L = -Σ<sub>t</sub> <span class="loss-eq-del">1 ·</span> log π(y*<sub>t</sub> | y*<sub>&lt;t</sub>, x)
      <br>
      <span style="font-size:.75rem;color:var(--muted)">implicit weight: 1/π → ∞ as p → 0</span>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="loss-box dft">
    <div class="loss-box-head">Dynamic Fine-Tuning (DFT)</div>
    <div class="loss-box-body">
      L = -Σ<sub>t</sub> <span class="loss-eq-add">sg(π(y*<sub>t</sub>)) ·</span> log π(y*<sub>t</sub> | y*<sub>&lt;t</sub>, x)
      <br>
      <span style="font-size:.75rem;color:var(--muted)">effective weight: p · (1/p) = 1 always</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>In PyTorch, this is literally one line of difference:</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">dft/train.py — the actual code change</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <pre style="font-family:var(--ff-mono);font-size:.8rem;color:#abb2bf;line-height:1.7;background:transparent;padding:0;margin:0;white-space:pre">
<span style="color:#5c6370;font-style:italic"># Standard SFT loss</span>
loss = <span style="color:#61afef">-log_probs</span>.<span style="color:#61afef">mean</span>()

<span style="color:#5c6370;font-style:italic"># DFT: one line change  ↓</span>
loss = <span style="color:#98c379">-(log_probs.exp().detach() * log_probs)</span>.<span style="color:#61afef">mean</span>()
<span style="color:#5c6370;font-style:italic">#       └── sg(p) ──────┘   └── log p ──┘</span>
<span style="color:#5c6370;font-style:italic">#       This is: -p * log p  (entropy-shaped, but p is stopped)</span></pre>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The result is -p·log(p) per token with a stop-gradient on p. Because sg prevents gradients from flowing through the weight, the optimization signal is the same as standard cross-entropy — just rebalanced. The rectified reward is now uniformly 1 for all correct tokens, mirroring RLVR (DeepSeek-R1's "give 1 to all correct samples") without any sampling or reward model.</p>

<h3>What about sentence-level weighting?</h3>
<p>A natural question: why not scale the whole sentence by its probability? The paper tries this and finds it fails: full sequence probabilities are so tiny they make the loss nearly zero. A geometric-mean variant (inspired by GSPO) is more stable but still weak. Token-level weighting is what works — raising average accuracy on Qwen2.5-Math-1.5B from 15.92 → 31.58 vs. the sentence-level variants which barely move the needle.</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ §04 CONVERGENCE -->
<section id="s-curves" data-code="curves">
<div class="section-no">04 — Convergence</div>
<h2>DFT Converges Faster and Flies Higher</h2>
<p>Looking at training dynamics on Qwen2.5-Math-1.5B, DFT shows three distinct advantages over SFT: faster convergence (peaks within the first 120 steps), better early performance (beats SFT's final best by step 10–20), and higher sample efficiency overall.</p>
<p>The effect is most dramatic on hard benchmarks. On OlympiadBench, SFT actually <em>degrades</em> performance — dropping from 15.88% to 12.63% — while DFT climbs to 27.08%. The model under SFT is memorizing training patterns; when it encounters harder olympiad problems it fails. DFT, with its balanced gradient signal, learns the underlying reasoning strategy.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Qwen2.5-Math-1.5B — accuracy vs. training steps</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">hover to read values</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div style="display:flex;gap:.75rem;margin-bottom:.75rem">
      <button class="cs-tab active" id="curve-olympiad" style="padding:.3rem .7rem;font-size:.7rem">OlympiadBench</button>
      <button class="cs-tab" id="curve-avg" style="padding:.3rem .7rem;font-size:.7rem">Average</button>
      <button class="cs-tab" id="curve-amc" style="padding:.3rem .7rem;font-size:.7rem">AMC 2023</button>
