  </div>
</div>

<p>Notice the green DFT line crosses SFT's best performance (dashed) in the very first handful of steps. SFT sometimes never recovers from the early gradient distortion, oscillating or declining on harder benchmarks throughout training.</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ §05 TOKEN POLARITY -->
<section id="s-polarity" data-code="polarity">
<div class="section-no">05 — Token Polarity</div>
<h2>SFT Shifts Right; DFT Polarizes</h2>
<p>Training with DFT doesn't just improve numbers — it changes what the model <em>does</em> with probability mass. Examining the distribution of token probabilities across the training set after training reveals a striking contrast:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>SFT</strong>: uniformly shifts probabilities toward higher confidence — the whole distribution slides right</li>
  <li><strong>DFT</strong>: creates a bimodal distribution — it increases some probabilities and <em>actively decreases others</em></li>
</ul>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">token probability distribution — before and after training</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click method to compare</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div style="display:flex;gap:.75rem;margin-bottom:.875rem">
      <button class="cs-tab active" id="dist-base">Base model</button>
      <button class="cs-tab" id="dist-sft">After SFT</button>
      <button class="cs-tab" id="dist-dft">After DFT</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="dist-canvas" style="width:100%;height:200px;display:block"></canvas>
    <div id="dist-caption" style="margin-top:.75rem;font-size:.75rem;color:var(--muted);font-family:var(--ff-mono)">
      Base model: roughly uniform distribution of token confidences
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The tokens being suppressed by DFT are revealing: inspecting the lowest-probability bin shows words like "the", "let", "," and "." — conjunctions and punctuation. DFT is learning to deprioritize grammatical scaffolding tokens and focus weight on semantically meaningful ones. This is exactly the "don't try to fit everything with equal confidence" principle that RL methods naturally discover through reward signals.</p>
<p>Other RL methods (DPO, GRPO, PPO) show the same polarization trend as DFT, though at smaller scale. Standard SFT is the outlier — it's the only method that treats every token as equally important.</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ §06 RESULTS -->
<section id="s-results" data-code="results">
<div class="section-no">06 — Results</div>
<h2>Beating RL Methods Without RL</h2>
<p>The punchline: DFT in the SFT setting (no reward model, no sampling, no reference model) averages <strong>31.58</strong> on math benchmarks for Qwen2.5-Math-1.5B. That's higher than GRPO (32.00 — though with offline DFT pushing to 35.43 it beats everything). Across all five model families tested, DFT's improvement is 1.4×–5.9× larger than standard SFT's gain.</p>

