<p>DFT in the pure SFT setting (no rewards) essentially matches GRPO while requiring no reference model, no batch sampling, and no reward verification. The offline RL variant (using rejected-sampling labels) beats everything by +3.43 over GRPO. The gains generalize: code generation (Qwen2.5-Coder-7B reaches 67.7 on HumanEval vs. SFT's 54.9), multimodal reasoning (MathVerse +3.71 vs. SFT's +1.83), and across model families from LLaMA-3.2-3B to Qwen2.5-Math-7B.</p>

<h3>Where DFT doesn't help</h3>
<p>One honest limitation: on factual knowledge tasks (Natural Questions), DFT actually <em>hurts</em> — SFT improves from 31.24% to 36.62% while DFT drops to 30.14%. The mechanism is revealing: DFT reinforces the model's existing beliefs. When the model lacks the knowledge being trained (new facts vs. new reasoning), its low-confidence predictions don't need to be suppressed — they need to be learned. DFT is most powerful when the task aligns with the model's prior competence, like logical reasoning and structured computation.</p>

